#
# **Every door reports here, and there is one of these because every door answers the same
# way** — the uniform shape paying for itself. Four return conventions and this could not exist.
#
# It records the **attempt**, not the change. What changed is in the row; this holds who asked,
# for what, and what they were told, including the refusals.
#
# **Recorded after the transaction, never inside it.** An entry written inside is erased by
# the rollback it was meant to explain, so a refused or failed attempt would leave no trace at
# exactly the moment a trace matters. The cost of writing it outside is a crash between commit
# and record, which loses one entry; the cost of writing it inside is losing every entry that
# describes a failure.
module AuditLog
  # What happened, as a value. A shape, so it holds no record and compares by value.
  class Entry < Shape
    def initialize(operation:, outcome:, actor_id: nil, error: nil, arguments: {})
      @operation = typed(operation, String)
      @outcome = typed(outcome, Symbol)
      # **Not an Integer.** A UUID primary key is an ordinary Rails choice, and asserting
      # Integer here raised *after* the transaction committed — telling the caller the
      # command had failed when it had already succeeded.
      @actor_id = typed(actor_id, String, allow_nil: true)
      @error = typed(error, Symbol, allow_nil: true)
      @arguments = typed(arguments, Hash)
    end

    attr_reader :operation, :outcome, :actor_id, :error, :arguments
  end

  # **Arguments are recorded, with the personal ones redacted.**
  #
  # An audit log is the classic place personal data leaks: written on every call, kept longer
  # than the rows it describes, copied into log aggregation, and visited by no erasure request
  # ever written. So two things are held back, and the first costs nobody anything:
  #
  # 1. **Anything `PersonalData` already declares.** That registry names every personal column
  #    and a guard keeps it from going stale, so an argument named for one is redacted without
  #    anybody deciding again. It is a floor that cannot be forgotten.
  # 2. **Anything the operation lists in `EXCLUDE_FROM_AUDIT`** — a token, a free-text note, a
  #    payload. For what is not a column, and so not in the registry.
  #
  # A value is never recorded, only that the argument was present: `email: "[redacted]"`.
  # **A broken sink does not fail the command.** The write has already committed by the time
  # this runs, so raising here would tell the caller the operation failed when it had
  # succeeded — the audit log deciding the outcome of the thing it is auditing. The failure
  # is not swallowed: it goes to stderr, where a silent audit trail would otherwise be the
  # one thing nobody notices.
  def self.record(operation:, outcome:, actor: nil, error: nil, arguments: {}, excluded: [])
    entry(operation: operation, outcome: outcome, actor: actor, error: error,
          arguments: arguments, excluded: excluded)
  rescue StandardError => error
    warn("audit log failed: #{error.class}: #{error.message}")
  end

  def self.entry(operation:, outcome:, actor:, error:, arguments:, excluded:)
    sink.call(
      Entry.new(
        operation: operation.to_s,
        outcome: outcome,
        actor_id: actor.respond_to?(:id) ? actor.id&.to_s : nil,
        error: error,
        arguments: redact(arguments, excluded),
      ),
    )
  end

  REDACTED = "[redacted]"

  # Personal by declaration, never by inference — the same position
  # `personal-data-is-declared-and-erasable` takes about columns. An argument is redacted
  # because `PersonalData` names a column of that name, or because the operation said so.
  def self.redact(arguments, excluded)
    arguments.to_h do |name, value|
      [name.to_sym, personal?(name, excluded) ? REDACTED : value]
    end
  end

  def self.personal?(name, excluded)
    return true if excluded.map(&:to_sym).include?(name.to_sym)
    return false unless defined?(::PersonalData)

    ::PersonalData::COLUMNS.any? { |_table, columns| columns.key?(name.to_s) }
  end

  private_class_method :entry, :redact, :personal?

  # **The default is a log line, and a log line is not an audit trail.** It works on the day
  # you install this and it is not what you want by the time anybody asks for the trail: logs
  # rotate, they are not queryable, and they are the first thing to be shipped somewhere with
  # a different retention policy. Point this at a table.
  #
  #   AuditLog.sink = ->(entry) { AuditEntryRecord.create!(**entry.to_h) }
  # **Never silent, even with no Rails loaded.** These base classes are POROs and must load
  # in a suite that never booted the framework, so the default falls back to stderr rather
  # than to nothing — a `nil` logger would make the audit trail disappear exactly where
  # nobody is watching for it, which is `nothing-fails-quietly` broken by default.
  def self.sink
    @sink ||= lambda do |entry|
      line = "audit #{entry.inspect}"

      defined?(::Rails) && ::Rails.respond_to?(:logger) ? ::Rails.logger.info(line) : warn(line)
    end
  end

  class << self
    attr_writer :sink
  end
end
